---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "@/components/nav.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/footer.astro";
---

<Layout title="Wazuh SIEM Home Lab | Alfayo Robin">
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-12 text-[var(--white)]">
    <a
      href="/#projects"
      class="mb-8 inline-block text-sm text-[var(--sec)] transition hover:opacity-80"
    >
      ← Back to Projects
    </a>

    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)]">
        Cybersecurity • Home Lab
      </p>

      <h1 class="mb-5 text-3xl font-bold md:text-5xl">
        Wazuh SIEM Home Lab
      </h1>

      <p class="max-w-3xl text-base leading-7 opacity-80 md:text-lg">
        A hands-on lab exploring security monitoring with Wazuh, using a
        virtual environment to generate and review security events.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Wazuh</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">VirtualBox</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Kali Linux</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Security Monitoring</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">1. Project Overview</h2>
      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        The aim of this project was to gain practical experience with a Security
        Information and Event Management (SIEM) platform. I installed Wazuh in
        VirtualBox, configured the lab environment, and worked through
        connectivity and setup issues before accessing the dashboard and
        reviewing generated security alerts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">2. Lab Environment</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        <li><strong>Virtualization:</strong> Oracle VirtualBox.</li>
        <li><strong>SIEM platform:</strong> Wazuh.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing environment:</strong> Kali Linux and Metasploitable, where applicable.</li>
        <li><strong>Objective:</strong> Access the Wazuh dashboard and review security events generated in the lab.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm opacity-70">
        Note: Update this list to reflect the exact virtual machines and network
        configuration you used.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">3. Setup and Configuration</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        <li>
          <strong>Prepared the environment.</strong>
          VirtualBox and Kali Linux were already installed, so I continued with
          setting up the Wazuh virtual machine.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Installed Wazuh.</strong>
          I followed the Wazuh installation process and recorded the network
          details needed to reach its dashboard.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Checked network connectivity.</strong>
          I investigated why the dashboard IP address was not initially
          accessible, including checking the virtual machine's network adapter
          configuration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accessed the dashboard.</strong>
          After troubleshooting the setup, I reached the Wazuh dashboard and
          continued exploring its monitoring features.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reviewed security events.</strong>
          I generated or observed lab activity and reviewed the resulting
          alerts in Wazuh.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">4. Screenshots and Evidence</h2>
      <p class="mb-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
       Screenshots demonstrating the installation, network
        configuration, dashboard access, and alerts observed during testing.
        
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <div class="flex min-h-40 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm opacity-60">Screenshot 1: Wazuh VM or installation</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-75">
            Evidence of the Wazuh virtual machine and installation.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <div class="flex min-h-40 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm opacity-60">Screenshot 2: Network configuration</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-75">
            Network settings or connectivity checks used during troubleshooting.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <div class="flex min-h-40 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm opacity-60">Screenshot 3: Wazuh dashboard</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-75">
            The dashboard after successful access.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <div class="flex min-h-40 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm opacity-60">Screenshot 4: Security alerts</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-75">
            Alerts or event details observed during lab testing.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">5. Challenges and Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        One of the main challenges was reaching the Wazuh dashboard using its
        assigned IP address. I investigated the VirtualBox network adapter and
        connectivity settings, then continued troubleshooting until I could
        access the dashboard and observe alerts. This helped me understand the
        importance of network configuration when building a security
        monitoring lab.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">6. Skills and Lessons Learned</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        <li>Installing and exploring a SIEM platform in a virtual lab.</li>
        <li>Investigating virtual network connectivity issues.</li>
        <li>Accessing security dashboards and reviewing alerts.</li>
        <li>Building practical foundations in security monitoring and event investigation.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-6">
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-semibold">7. Conclusion</h2>
      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        This project gave me practical exposure to setting up a security
        monitoring environment and reviewing events through Wazuh. Troubleshooting
        connectivity also strengthened my understanding of virtual networking.
        I plan to continue expanding the lab by connecting monitored systems and
        investigating additional security events.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>